{
	"id": "g5.maths.convert_units",
	"lesson": [
		{
			"text": "Quick memory: in grade 4 you met measuring and converting. How many centimetres are in 1 metre? 100."
		},
		{
			"text": "Metric units go up in tens, hundreds and thousands: 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 km = 1,000 m, 1 kg = 1,000 g and 1 L = 1,000 mL.",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "table",
				"rows": [
					[
						"big unit",
						"small units"
					],
					[
						"1 cm",
						"10 mm"
					],
					[
						"1 m",
						"100 cm"
					],
					[
						"1 km",
						"1,000 m"
					],
					[
						"1 kg",
						"1,000 g"
					],
					[
						"1 L",
						"1,000 mL"
					]
				]
			}
		},
		{
			"text": "From a big unit to a small one, multiply: 3 m = 3 × 100 = 300 cm. From small to big, divide: 2,500 g = 2,500 ÷ 1,000 = 2.5 kg.",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "table",
				"rows": [
					[
						"From",
						"To",
						"Do"
					],
					[
						"m",
						"cm",
						"× 100"
					],
					[
						"cm",
						"m",
						"÷ 100"
					],
					[
						"g",
						"kg",
						"÷ 1,000"
					],
					[
						"kg",
						"g",
						"× 1,000"
					]
				]
			}
		},
		{
			"text": "To compare, change them to the same unit first: 1.2 m = 120 cm, which is more than 95 cm."
		},
		{
			"text": "A line plot shows measurements as marks above a number line. You can add them up, or share the total out equally.",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "line_plot",
				"den": 4,
				"from": 0,
				"to": 4,
				"counts": {
					"1": 1,
					"2": 2,
					"3": 1
				},
				"unit": "m"
			}
		},
		{
			"text": "Ribbons of 1/4 m, 1/2 m and 3/4 m: 1/4 + 2/4 + 3/4 = 6/4 m, which is 1 1/2 m in all."
		},
		{
			"text": "Try it in the world: each block in your world is 1 m. How many centimetres tall is a tower of 3 blocks? And how many metres is a 2 km walk? Next: volume with cubes."
		}
	],
	"exercises": [
